IMPORT prelude

-- Customs Act 1901 (Commonwealth) -- coming home through the airport: must I fill in the incoming passenger card, what
-- must I declare on it, what may I bring in duty-free, what happens to goods that are prohibited, and what can I be made
-- to pay for a false card or for smuggling?
--
-- SEAT: the incoming traveller (a passenger arriving in Australia on an international flight or voyage with accompanied
-- baggage). Every decision is phrased as that traveller asks it, from facts they could state. Where the Act or the
-- Regulation speaks to an officer or a Collector (ss 68, 71AAAB, 195, 229, 243X; reg 27), the encoding says what it means
-- for the traveller: what they must give, what may be taken from them, what they may be asked to pay.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 4(1) ("Smuggling"); ss 50-51 (prohibited imports); s 68(1)(d), (2)-(4) (entry of imported goods;
-- a passenger's personal effects are not entered); ss 71, 71AAAA, 71AAAB (information about Subdivision AA goods -- the
-- passenger card); s 195 (questions to passengers); s 229(1)(a), (b), (q) (forfeiture); s 233(1)(a), (b), (d) and s 233AB
-- (smuggling; importing prohibited imports; penalties); s 233BABAD (tobacco imported to defraud the revenue); s 234(1)(a),
-- (d), (2), (3) (evading duty; false statements to an officer); s 243T and s 243U (strict-liability false statements, with
-- and without loss of duty; the error notice; the passenger exclusion in s 243U(5)(b)); s 243X (infringement notices).
--
-- Edition: Customs Act 1901, compilation register id C2026C00378 (five volumes), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/C1901A00006.txt (retrieved 2026-09-22).
-- Instruments used:
--   Customs Regulation 2015, Compilation No. 38, compilation date 1 July 2026 ("includes amendments F2026L00873"; register
--   id F2026C00589), as deposited at ../../../customs-regulation-2015/registers/source-bundle/F2015L00375.txt (retrieved
--   2026-10-19): s 25 (goods exempt from entry), s 26 ($1 000), s 27 (the incoming passenger card and its questions), s 27A
--   (the Australia Travel Declaration), s 133 (payment periods), Schedule 8 items 63, 65, 80, 81 (provisions open to an
--   infringement notice).
--   Customs Tariff Act 1995, compilation register id C2026C00399, as deposited at
--   ../../../customs-tariff-act-1995/registers/source-bundle/C2004A04997.txt (retrieved 2026-09-22): Schedule 4 item 15
--   (goods "as prescribed by by-law" imported by passengers: Free) and s 18 (a concessional item applies only if it gives
--   less duty). The plan named item 21; in this compilation item 21 is goods imported for repair and re-export.
--   Crimes Act 1914 s 4AA (penalty unit: "$330 (subject to indexation under subsection (3))", first indexation day 1 July
--   2026), as deposited at ../../../crimes-act-1914/registers/source-bundle/C1914A00012.txt. The indexed figure is not in
--   the deposit, so the value of a penalty unit is an INPUT.
--   The Customs By-laws that fill in Schedule 4 item 15 (the passenger concession: how much alcohol, tobacco and other goods
--   are free, and for whom) are NOT deposited: those amounts and conditions are INPUTS. So are the Customs (Prohibited
--   Imports) Regulations 1956, which say which goods are prohibited: the prohibition is an INPUT.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: cargo, post and commercial importers (entries, warehousing, self-assessed clearance, low value goods);
-- the import declaration's contents and charges (ss 71A-71B); duty rates and their indexation (Customs Tariff Schedule 3,
-- s 19) -- the duty is an INPUT; how the By-laws treat goods over the concession; tier 1 and tier 2 goods (ss 233BAA,
-- 233BAB), narcotic goods, restricted goods (s 233BABAE); search, detention and seizure powers (Part XII); the
-- prosecution and the Court's choice of penalty (Part XIV); the outgoing traveller. The Biosecurity Act 2015 declaration
-- on the same card is a separate duty under a separate Act and is not here; nor is the Migration Act part of the card.
-- Chloe's 2011 return is answered on the 2026 text: the Customs Regulation 2015 did not exist in 2011, and the earlier
-- regulations are not deposited.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill from the individual's seat with the design defaults ruled on
-- 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. NOT for public use as advice.
-- Judgments the law hands to an officer or a court are INPUTS ("if the Collector reasonably suspects ...", "where the
-- Court can determine the amount of the duty ..."). Money is in dollars.

§ `Customs Act 1901`

§§ `s 68(1)(d), (4) -- are my bags my personal effects`

-- s 68(1): "This section applies to: (a) goods that are imported into Australia ... but does not apply to: (d) goods that
-- are accompanied or unaccompanied personal or household effects of a passenger, or a member of a crew, of a ship or
-- aircraft".
-- s 68(4): "For the purposes of paragraph (1)(d), goods: (a) in quantities exceeding what could reasonably be expected to
-- be required by a passenger or member of the crew of a ship or aircraft for his or her own use; or (b) that are, to the
-- knowledge or belief of a passenger or member of the crew of a ship or aircraft, to be sold, or used in the course of
-- trading, in Australia; are not included in the personal or household effects of a passenger or crew member."
-- "Personal or household effects" is not defined; whether the goods are such effects is asked as a fact.
@export Are the goods I am carrying my personal or household effects, so that I need not enter them with an import declaration (s 68(1)(d), (4))?
GIVEN `the goods are personal or household effects` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the goods are your personal or household effects (clothes, gifts, souvenirs, things for your own use)
      `more than I could reasonably need for my own use` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the quantity is more than a traveller could reasonably be expected to need for their own use
      `I know or believe they will be sold or traded in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you know or believe the goods are to be sold, or used in trading, in Australia
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Are my goods my personal effects` IF
        `the goods are personal or household effects`
    AND (NOT `more than I could reasonably need for my own use`)
    AND (NOT `I know or believe they will be sold or traded in Australia`)

§§ `s 68(2), (3), reg 25, reg 26 -- must I enter my goods`

-- s 68(3): "If the owner of goods to which this section applies does not enter the goods under subsection (2), the owner
-- must enter the goods for home consumption or, for goods other than tobacco products, enter the goods for warehousing".
-- reg 25: "For paragraph 68(1)(i) of the Act, goods are exempt from section 68 of the Act if the goods: (a) would, but for
-- subsection 68(4) of the Act, be accompanied personal or household effects of a passenger ...; and (b) have a value not
-- exceeding $250 or such other amount as is prescribed for subparagraph 68(1)(f)(iii) of the Act."
-- reg 26: "For subparagraph 68(1)(f)(iii) of the Act, the amount is $1 000."
-- So goods that fall out of s 68(1)(d) only because of s 68(4) are still free of entry up to $1 000 (value worked out
-- under Part VIII Division 2, asked here as a fact).
DECLARE `Entry outcome` IS ONE OF
    `no entry needed: my personal effects (s 68(1)(d))`
    `no entry needed: exempt by reg 25 (value not over 1000 dollars)`
    `I must enter them for home consumption or warehousing (s 68(3))`
    `I must enter them for home consumption; tobacco cannot be warehoused (s 68(3))`

@export Must I enter the goods I am carrying with an import declaration (s 68, Customs Regulation 2015 regs 25-26)?
GIVEN `the goods are personal or household effects` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the goods are your personal or household effects
      `more than I could reasonably need for my own use` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the quantity is more than you could reasonably need for your own use
      `I know or believe they will be sold or traded in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you know or believe they are to be sold or traded in Australia
      `the customs value of the goods` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of the goods in dollars, worked out under Part VIII Division 2
      `the goods are tobacco products` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the goods are tobacco products (cigarettes, cigars, tobacco)
GIVETH AN `Entry outcome`
DECIDE `Must I enter my goods` IS
    IF `Are my goods my personal effects` `the goods are personal or household effects` `more than I could reasonably need for my own use` `I know or believe they will be sold or traded in Australia`
    THEN `no entry needed: my personal effects (s 68(1)(d))`
    ELSE IF `the goods are personal or household effects` AND `the customs value of the goods` <= 1000
         THEN `no entry needed: exempt by reg 25 (value not over 1000 dollars)`
         ELSE IF `the goods are tobacco products`
              THEN `I must enter them for home consumption; tobacco cannot be warehoused (s 68(3))`
              ELSE `I must enter them for home consumption or warehousing (s 68(3))`

§§ `ss 71, 71AAAA, 71AAAB, reg 27, reg 27A -- must I fill in the incoming passenger card`

-- s 71AAAA: "Subdivision AA goods means: (a) goods of a kind referred to in paragraph 68(1)(d)".
-- s 71AAAB(1): "A person: (a) who is the owner of Subdivision AA goods ... must, in the circumstances specified in the
-- regulations, provide, under section 71, the information specified in the regulations".
-- reg 27(3): "The specified circumstance in which the information is to be given is that a Collector has requested the
-- person to give the information." reg 27(5) Note: the form "if the person giving the information is a passenger--the
-- incoming passenger card".
-- reg 27(6): "However, if a person has complied with paragraph 27A(2)(a) in relation to the goods, a Collector may request
-- the person to give the information ... only if: (a) a decision has not been made about the goods under subsection
-- 71AAAB(2) of the Act; and (b) the Collector reasonably suspects that the Australia Travel Declaration ... is not, or is no
-- longer, accurate or complete."
-- The duty is triggered by a Collector's request, not by arrival alone.
DECLARE `My arrival` HAS
    `I am a crew member` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you are a member of the crew rather than a passenger
    `I am bringing accompanied personal or household effects` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you have personal or household effects with you
    `a Collector has asked me for the information` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether a Collector (an officer) has asked you to give the information, for example by handing you the card
    `I presented my Australia Travel Declaration message` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you showed the electronic message confirming your Australia Travel Declaration (reg 27A(2)(a))
    `a decision about my goods has already been made` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether a Collector has already authorised or refused delivery of your goods under s 71AAAB(2)
    `the Collector reasonably suspects my Travel Declaration is inaccurate` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether the Collector reasonably suspects your Australia Travel Declaration is not, or no longer, accurate or complete
    `my Travel Declaration is inaccurate or incomplete` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether your Australia Travel Declaration is in fact not, or no longer, accurate or complete
    `millilitres of alcoholic beverages` IS A NUMBER
    @desc How many millilitres of alcoholic drinks you are bringing
    `cigarettes` IS A NUMBER
    @desc How many cigarettes you are bringing
    `grams of other tobacco products` IS A NUMBER
    @desc How many grams of tobacco products other than cigarettes you are bringing
    `value of goods bought overseas or duty-free` IS A NUMBER
    @desc Combined value in dollars of goods obtained overseas or bought duty free or tax free in Australia (gifts included)
    `I have goods that are prohibited or restricted` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you have goods that are prohibited or subject to restrictions (medicines, steroids, firearms, weapons, illicit drugs ...)
    `I have business or commercial samples` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you have samples intended for business or commercial use

@export Must I fill in the incoming passenger card (Customs Act s 71AAAB, Customs Regulation 2015 regs 27(3), 27(6))?
GIVEN `my arrival` IS A `My arrival` @desc The facts of your arrival
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I fill in the incoming passenger card` IF
        `my arrival`'s `I am bringing accompanied personal or household effects`
    AND (NOT `my arrival`'s `I am a crew member`)
    AND `my arrival`'s `a Collector has asked me for the information`
    AND (    (NOT `my arrival`'s `I presented my Australia Travel Declaration message`)
         OR  (    (NOT `my arrival`'s `a decision about my goods has already been made`)
              AND `my arrival`'s `the Collector reasonably suspects my Travel Declaration is inaccurate`))

-- reg 27A(2)(b): the person must, "if the Australia Travel Declaration is not, or is no longer, accurate or complete--
-- provide any information required to correct or to complete the declaration to a Collector orally or in writing".
@export Must I correct my Australia Travel Declaration to a Collector (Customs Regulation 2015 reg 27A(2)(b))?
GIVEN `my arrival` IS A `My arrival` @desc The facts of your arrival
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I correct my Travel Declaration` IF
        `my arrival`'s `I presented my Australia Travel Declaration message`
    AND `my arrival`'s `my Travel Declaration is inaccurate or incomplete`

§§ `reg 27(2) -- what the card asks me to declare`

-- reg 27(2): "(e) whether the goods are prohibited or subject to restrictions; (f) whether the goods consist of more than
-- 2,250 millilitres of alcoholic beverages; (g) whether the goods consist of more than 25 cigarettes or more than 25 grams
-- of tobacco products; (h) for goods obtained overseas or purchased as duty free or tax free goods in Australia--whether
-- the goods have a combined total value of: (i) if the person is a passenger--more than $900; and (ii) if the person is a
-- crew member--more than $450; (i) whether the goods are samples intended for business or commercial use."
-- These are the card's thresholds, set in the deposited Regulation. They are the thresholds for DECLARING; the duty-free
-- concession itself is set by the By-laws (below).
@export Must I answer YES to the alcohol question on the card (more than 2,250 mL; reg 27(2)(f))?
GIVEN `my arrival` IS A `My arrival` @desc The facts of your arrival
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I declare my alcohol` IF
    `my arrival`'s `millilitres of alcoholic beverages` > 2250

@export Must I answer YES to the tobacco question on the card (more than 25 cigarettes or 25 grams; reg 27(2)(g))?
GIVEN `my arrival` IS A `My arrival` @desc The facts of your arrival
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I declare my tobacco` IF
        `my arrival`'s `cigarettes` > 25
    OR  `my arrival`'s `grams of other tobacco products` > 25

@export Must I answer YES to the question about goods bought overseas or duty-free (over 900 dollars, or 450 for crew; reg 27(2)(h))?
GIVEN `my arrival` IS A `My arrival` @desc The facts of your arrival
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I declare my overseas purchases` IF
    IF `my arrival`'s `I am a crew member`
    THEN `my arrival`'s `value of goods bought overseas or duty-free` > 450
    ELSE `my arrival`'s `value of goods bought overseas or duty-free` > 900

@export Do I have anything to declare for customs on the incoming passenger card (reg 27(2)(e)-(i))?
GIVEN `my arrival` IS A `My arrival` @desc The facts of your arrival
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I have anything to declare for customs` IF
        `my arrival`'s `I have goods that are prohibited or restricted`
    OR  `Must I declare my alcohol` `my arrival`
    OR  `Must I declare my tobacco` `my arrival`
    OR  `Must I declare my overseas purchases` `my arrival`
    OR  `my arrival`'s `I have business or commercial samples`

§§ `Customs Tariff Act 1995 Schedule 4 item 15, s 18 -- what may I bring in duty-free`

-- Customs Tariff Act 1995 Schedule 4 item 15: "Goods, as prescribed by by-law, that are: (a) goods imported by passengers
-- or members of the crew of ships or aircraft; ... (e) passengers' personal effects, furniture or household goods" --
-- rate of duty "Free". s 18(1): an item in Schedule 4 "only applies to those goods if the duty payable in respect of those
-- goods under that item is less than the duty that ... would be payable" otherwise; "Free" always is, for dutiable goods.
-- WHAT the By-laws prescribe (how many cigarettes, how much alcohol, what value of other goods, which travellers qualify,
-- and what happens above the limit) is NOT in the deposit. Each is an INPUT here.
DECLARE `By-law concession` HAS
    `cigarettes free under the by-law` IS A NUMBER
    @desc The number of cigarettes the Customs By-law lets you bring duty-free (not deposited; e.g. the plan's 25)
    `millilitres of alcohol free under the by-law` IS A NUMBER
    @desc The millilitres of alcoholic drinks the Customs By-law lets you bring duty-free (not deposited)
    `value of general goods free under the by-law` IS A NUMBER
    @desc The dollar value of other goods the Customs By-law lets you bring duty-free (not deposited; e.g. the plan's 900)
    `I qualify for the alcohol and tobacco concession` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you meet the by-law's conditions for the alcohol and tobacco concession (for example any age condition)

@export How many of my cigarettes come in duty-free under the passenger concession (Customs Tariff Sch 4 item 15, with the by-law figure)?
GIVEN `my arrival` IS A `My arrival` @desc The facts of your arrival
      `the by-law` IS A `By-law concession` @desc The by-law's concession figures (not deposited; supply them)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many of my cigarettes are duty-free` IS
    IF (NOT `the by-law`'s `I qualify for the alcohol and tobacco concession`)
    THEN 0
    ELSE IF `my arrival`'s `cigarettes` < `the by-law`'s `cigarettes free under the by-law`
         THEN `my arrival`'s `cigarettes`
         ELSE `the by-law`'s `cigarettes free under the by-law`

@export How many cigarettes must I pay duty on?
GIVEN `my arrival` IS A `My arrival` @desc The facts of your arrival
      `the by-law` IS A `By-law concession` @desc The by-law's concession figures (not deposited; supply them)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many cigarettes must I pay duty on` IS
    `my arrival`'s `cigarettes` - `How many of my cigarettes are duty-free` `my arrival` `the by-law`

@export How much of my alcohol (in millilitres) comes in duty-free under the passenger concession?
GIVEN `my arrival` IS A `My arrival` @desc The facts of your arrival
      `the by-law` IS A `By-law concession` @desc The by-law's concession figures (not deposited; supply them)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much of my alcohol is duty-free` IS
    IF (NOT `the by-law`'s `I qualify for the alcohol and tobacco concession`)
    THEN 0
    ELSE IF `my arrival`'s `millilitres of alcoholic beverages` < `the by-law`'s `millilitres of alcohol free under the by-law`
         THEN `my arrival`'s `millilitres of alcoholic beverages`
         ELSE `the by-law`'s `millilitres of alcohol free under the by-law`

-- Whether goods ABOVE the general limit lose the concession entirely or only on the excess is for the By-law, not
-- deposited; only "within the limit" is encoded.
@export Are my other goods (bought overseas or duty-free) within the passenger concession's value limit?
GIVEN `my arrival` IS A `My arrival` @desc The facts of your arrival
      `the by-law` IS A `By-law concession` @desc The by-law's concession figures (not deposited; supply them)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Are my other goods within the concession` IF
    `my arrival`'s `value of goods bought overseas or duty-free` <= `the by-law`'s `value of general goods free under the by-law`

§§ `ss 50, 51, 229(1)(b) -- are my goods prohibited imports, and what happens to them`

-- s 50(2): the power to prohibit "may be exercised: (a) by prohibiting the importation of goods absolutely; (aa) by
-- prohibiting the importation of goods in specified circumstances; (b) by prohibiting the importation of goods from a
-- specified place; or (c) by prohibiting the importation of goods unless specified conditions or restrictions are
-- complied with." s 51(1): "Goods, the importation of which is prohibited under section 50, are prohibited imports."
-- s 229(1): "The following goods shall be forfeited to the Crown: ... (b) All prohibited imports."
-- Which goods the Customs (Prohibited Imports) Regulations 1956 catch is not deposited: the kind of prohibition is an INPUT.
DECLARE `Prohibition` IS ONE OF
    `not prohibited`
    `prohibited absolutely`
    `prohibited in specified circumstances`
    `prohibited from a specified place`
    `prohibited unless conditions are complied with or a permission is held`

@export Are the goods I am bringing prohibited imports (ss 50, 51)?
GIVEN `the prohibition` IS A `Prohibition` @desc How the Customs (Prohibited Imports) Regulations 1956 treat the goods (not deposited; supply it)
      `the circumstances, place or unmet condition applies to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a conditional prohibition: whether the circumstances apply, the goods come from the place, or you have not met the condition or do not hold the permission
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Are my goods prohibited imports` IF
    CONSIDER `the prohibition`
    WHEN `not prohibited` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `prohibited absolutely` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE `the circumstances, place or unmet condition applies to me`

@export Will my goods be forfeited to the Crown (s 229(1)(a), (b), (q))?
GIVEN `my goods are prohibited imports` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the goods are prohibited imports
      `my goods are smuggled or unlawfully imported` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the goods are smuggled or unlawfully imported
      `my goods are dutiable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether duty is payable on the goods (for example cigarettes above the concession)
      `I denied having dutiable goods or did not fully disclose them when questioned` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you denied having dutiable goods, or when questioned by an officer did not fully disclose them
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
-- s 229(1)(q): "All dutiable goods found in the possession or in the baggage of any person who has got out of, landed from
-- or gone on board any ship boat or aircraft and who has denied that he or she has any dutiable goods in his or her
-- possession, or who when questioned by an officer has not fully disclosed th[em]".
DECIDE `Will my goods be forfeited` IF
        `my goods are prohibited imports`
    OR  `my goods are smuggled or unlawfully imported`
    OR  (`my goods are dutiable` AND `I denied having dutiable goods or did not fully disclose them when questioned`)

§§ `s 195 -- must I answer the officer's questions`

-- s 195(1): "An officer of Customs may question: ... (b) any person who has, or who the officer has reason to believe has,
-- got off a ship or out of an aircraft ... as to whether that person or any child or other person accompanying him or her
-- has on his or her person, in his or her baggage or otherwise with him or her any: (d) dutiable goods; or (e) excisable
-- goods; or (f) prohibited goods." s 195(2): "A person shall answer questions put to him or her in pursuance of subsection
-- (1). Penalty: 30 penalty units." s 195(3): strict liability.
@export Must I answer a customs officer's questions about dutiable or prohibited goods (s 195)?
GIVEN `an officer is questioning me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a customs officer is questioning you after you got off the aircraft or ship
      `the questions are about dutiable, excisable or prohibited goods with me or my companions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the questions ask whether you (or a child or other person with you) have dutiable, excisable or prohibited goods
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I answer the officer's questions` IF
    `an officer is questioning me` AND `the questions are about dutiable, excisable or prohibited goods with me or my companions`

@export What is the most I can be fined for not answering the officer's questions, in penalty units (s 195(2))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined for not answering, in penalty units` IS 30

§§ `ss 234(1)(d), 243T, 243U -- is my false card an offence`

-- s 234(1): "A person shall not: ... (d) do any of the following: (i) intentionally make or cause to be made a statement to
-- an officer, reckless as to the fact that the statement is false or misleading in a material particular; (ii)
-- intentionally omit or cause to be omitted from a statement made to an officer any matter or thing, reckless as to the fact
-- that without the matter or thing the statement is misleading in a material particular".
-- s 243T(1): strict liability where the statement is false or misleading in a material particular and "the amount of duty
-- properly payable on the goods exceeds the amount of duty that would have been payable if the amount of duty were
-- determined on the basis that the statement was not false or misleading".
-- s 243U(1): the same where neither loss of duty nor excess refund results; but s 243U(5): "statement does not include:
-- ... (b) a statement that a person who is or was a passenger on, or a member of the crew of, a ship or aircraft made in
-- relation to his or her accompanied personal or household effects that were carried on the ship or aircraft."
-- s 243T(4), 243U(4): neither applies if the person "voluntarily gives written notice (an error notice) to an officer"
-- before a s 214AD notice; s 243T(4)(c) also needs the duty paid in full before an infringement notice or proceedings;
-- s 243T(4A), 243U(4A): not voluntary if given after an officer exercises a power to verify the statement.
-- s 243T(5), (6): no offence where the statement itself flags the uncertainty, identifies the information and gives reasons.
DECLARE `My statement` HAS
    `false or misleading in a material particular` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether what you told the officer (or wrote on the card) was false or misleading in a material particular, including by leaving something out
    `made to an officer` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether the statement was made to an officer (the card is handed to a Collector)
    `I made it intentionally` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you meant to make the statement (or leave the thing out)
    `I was reckless whether it was false or misleading` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you were aware of a substantial risk that it was false or misleading and took the risk unjustifiably
    `less duty would have been paid on it` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether, on the statement, less duty would be payable than is properly payable
    `it was about the amount of duty payable` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether the statement was about the amount of duty payable on particular goods (s 234(3))
    `it was about my accompanied personal effects` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you made it as a passenger or crew member about your accompanied personal or household effects
    `I gave a voluntary error notice in time` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you voluntarily gave an officer written notice of the error before any s 214AD notice and before an officer moved to verify it
    `I paid the duty in full before any infringement notice or proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you paid all the duty properly payable before an infringement notice or proceedings
    `it flagged my uncertainty, the information and my reasons` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether the statement itself said you were uncertain, identified the information and set out your reasons

@export Could my false statement or card be an offence under s 234(1)(d) (intentional, reckless as to falsity)?
GIVEN `the statement` IS A `My statement` @desc The facts about what you said or wrote
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Could my statement be an offence under s 234(1)(d)` IF
        `the statement`'s `false or misleading in a material particular`
    AND `the statement`'s `made to an officer`
    AND `the statement`'s `I made it intentionally`
    AND `the statement`'s `I was reckless whether it was false or misleading`

@export Could my false statement or card be the strict-liability offence in s 243T (false statement resulting in loss of duty)?
GIVEN `the statement` IS A `My statement` @desc The facts about what you said or wrote
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Could my statement be an offence under s 243T` IF
        `the statement`'s `false or misleading in a material particular`
    AND `the statement`'s `made to an officer`
    AND `the statement`'s `less duty would have been paid on it`
    AND (NOT (`the statement`'s `I gave a voluntary error notice in time` AND `the statement`'s `I paid the duty in full before any infringement notice or proceedings`))
    AND (NOT `the statement`'s `it flagged my uncertainty, the information and my reasons`)

@export Could my false statement or card be the strict-liability offence in s 243U (false statement not resulting in loss of duty)?
GIVEN `the statement` IS A `My statement` @desc The facts about what you said or wrote
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Could my statement be an offence under s 243U` IF
        `the statement`'s `false or misleading in a material particular`
    AND `the statement`'s `made to an officer`
    AND (NOT `the statement`'s `less duty would have been paid on it`)
    AND (NOT `the statement`'s `it was about my accompanied personal effects`)
    AND (NOT `the statement`'s `I gave a voluntary error notice in time`)

§§ `ss 234(2)(c), (3), 243T(3), 243U(3) -- what can I be fined for a false statement`

-- s 234(2)(c): "subject to subsection (3), in the case of an offence against paragraph (1)(d), by a penalty not exceeding
-- 250 penalty units". s 234(3): "Where a person is convicted of an offence against paragraph (1)(d) in relation to a
-- statement made ... in respect of the amount of duty payable on particular goods, a Court may ... impose a penalty not
-- exceeding the sum of 100 penalty units and twice the amount of the duty payable on those goods."
-- READING: "subject to subsection (3)" is read as subsection (3) setting the maximum in its case, in place of 250 penalty
-- units. A court could read (3) as an additional ceiling the prosecution may choose; that reading is not encoded.
@export What is the most I can be fined for a false statement under s 234(1)(d), in dollars (s 234(2)(c), (3))?
GIVEN `the statement` IS A `My statement` @desc The facts about what you said or wrote
      `the duty payable on those goods` IS A NUMBER @desc The duty payable on the goods, in dollars
      `a penalty unit in dollars` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of a penalty unit (Crimes Act s 4AA: 330 dollars before indexation)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined under s 234(1)(d)` IS
    IF `the statement`'s `it was about the amount of duty payable`
    THEN 100 * `a penalty unit in dollars` + 2 * `the duty payable on those goods`
    ELSE 250 * `a penalty unit in dollars`

-- s 243T(3): "punishable on conviction by a fine not exceeding the greater of: (a) 60 penalty units; and (b) the amount of
-- the excess."
@export What is the most I can be fined under s 243T, in dollars (the greater of 60 penalty units and the duty shortfall)?
GIVEN `the duty shortfall` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount by which the duty properly payable exceeds the duty on your statement, in dollars
      `a penalty unit in dollars` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of a penalty unit (Crimes Act s 4AA: 330 dollars before indexation)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined under s 243T` IS
    IF 60 * `a penalty unit in dollars` > `the duty shortfall`
    THEN 60 * `a penalty unit in dollars`
    ELSE `the duty shortfall`

-- s 243U(3): "an amount not exceeding 60 penalty units for each statement that is found by the court to be false or
-- misleading."
@export What is the most I can be fined under s 243U, in dollars (60 penalty units for each false statement)?
GIVEN `false statements` IS A NUMBER @desc How many statements the court finds false or misleading
      `a penalty unit in dollars` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of a penalty unit (Crimes Act s 4AA: 330 dollars before indexation)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined under s 243U` IS
    60 * `a penalty unit in dollars` * `false statements`

§§ `s 243X, reg 133, Schedule 8 -- what can an infringement notice ask me to pay`

-- s 243X(2): "The penalty must not exceed either: (a) one-quarter of the maximum fine that a court could impose on the
-- person as a penalty for that offence; or (b) subject to subsection (3), whichever of the following applies: (i) 15
-- penalty units if the person is an individual". s 243X(3): "Paragraph (2)(b) does not apply if: (a) the penalty for the
-- offence may be determined wholly or partly by reference to: (i) an amount of duty ...; or (ii) the value of particular
-- goods; and (b) it is possible to determine that amount or that value."
-- Schedule 8 of the Regulation lists s 233(1)(b) (item 63), s 233(1)(d) (item 65), s 243T(1) (item 80) and s 243U(1) (item
-- 81) among the provisions open to an infringement notice; s 234(1)(d) is not listed (and is not strict liability).
@export What is the most an infringement notice can ask me, an individual, to pay (s 243X(2), (3))?
GIVEN `the maximum fine a court could impose` IS A NUMBER @desc The court's maximum fine for the offence, in dollars
      `the penalty is set by reference to duty or value, and that can be determined` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offence's penalty is worked out from an amount of duty or the value of goods, and that amount or value can be determined
      `a penalty unit in dollars` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of a penalty unit (Crimes Act s 4AA: 330 dollars before indexation)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most an infringement notice can ask me to pay` IS
    IF `the penalty is set by reference to duty or value, and that can be determined`
    THEN `the maximum fine a court could impose` / 4
    ELSE IF `the maximum fine a court could impose` / 4 < 15 * `a penalty unit in dollars`
         THEN `the maximum fine a court could impose` / 4
         ELSE 15 * `a penalty unit in dollars`

-- reg 133(2) table: item 1, a notice given "in a section 234AA place" for "paragraph 233(1)(b) or (c), or subsection
-- 234A(1) or 234AB(3)": "The period that ends when the person leaves the place for the first time after the notice is
-- given"; item 2, s 243T(1): "The period that ends 28 days after the day the notice is given"; item 3, any other: 28 days.
DECLARE `Payment period` IS ONE OF
    `before I first leave the place where I was given the notice`
    `28 days after the day the notice is given`

DECLARE `Infringement offence` IS ONE OF
    `importing prohibited imports (s 233(1)(b))`
    `false statement with loss of duty (s 243T(1))`
    `false statement without loss of duty (s 243U(1))`

@export By when must I pay an infringement notice (Customs Regulation 2015 reg 133)?
GIVEN `the offence` IS AN `Infringement offence` @desc The offence the notice alleges
      `I was given it in the passenger processing area` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were given the notice in a section 234AA place (the signed passenger processing area)
GIVETH A `Payment period`
DECIDE `By when must I pay the infringement notice` IS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `importing prohibited imports (s 233(1)(b))` THEN
        IF `I was given it in the passenger processing area`
        THEN `before I first leave the place where I was given the notice`
        ELSE `28 days after the day the notice is given`
    OTHERWISE `28 days after the day the notice is given`

§§ `ss 4(1), 233, 233AB, 234(1)(a), 233BABAD -- smuggling and evading duty`

-- s 4(1): "Smuggling means any importation, introduction or exportation or attempted importation, introduction or
-- exportation of goods with intent to defraud the revenue."
@export Am I smuggling, in the Act's sense (s 4(1))?
GIVEN `I am importing or attempting to import the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are bringing, or trying to bring, the goods into Australia
      `I intend to defraud the revenue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you intend to avoid duty or tax that is owed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I smuggling` IF
    `I am importing or attempting to import the goods` AND `I intend to defraud the revenue`

-- s 233AB(1): for smuggling (s 233(1)(a)): "(a) where the Court can determine the amount of the duty ... a penalty not
-- exceeding 5 times the amount of that duty; or (b) where the Court cannot determine the amount of that duty, a penalty not
-- exceeding 1,000 penalty units."
@export What is the most I can be fined for smuggling, in dollars (s 233(1)(a), s 233AB(1))?
GIVEN `the court can determine the duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court can work out the duty that would have been payable
      `the duty` IS A NUMBER @desc The duty that would have been payable on the goods, in dollars
      `a penalty unit in dollars` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of a penalty unit (Crimes Act s 4AA: 330 dollars before indexation)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined for smuggling` IS
    IF `the court can determine the duty`
    THEN 5 * `the duty`
    ELSE 1000 * `a penalty unit in dollars`

-- s 233AB(2): for importing prohibited imports (s 233(1)(b)): "(a) where the Court can determine the value of the goods
-- ..., a penalty not exceeding: (i) 3 times the value of those goods; or (ii) 1,000 penalty units; whichever is the
-- greater; or (b) where the Court cannot determine the value of those goods--a penalty not exceeding 1,000 penalty units."
-- s 233(1AB): strict liability for s 233(1)(b).
@export What is the most I can be fined for bringing in prohibited imports, in dollars (s 233(1)(b), s 233AB(2))?
GIVEN `the court can determine the value` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court can work out the value of the goods
      `the value of the goods` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of the goods, in dollars
      `a penalty unit in dollars` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of a penalty unit (Crimes Act s 4AA: 330 dollars before indexation)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined for prohibited imports` IS
    IF `the court can determine the value` AND 3 * `the value of the goods` > 1000 * `a penalty unit in dollars`
    THEN 3 * `the value of the goods`
    ELSE 1000 * `a penalty unit in dollars`

-- s 234(2)(a): evading duty (s 234(1)(a)): "a penalty not exceeding 5 times the amount of that duty and not less than 2
-- times that amount; or (ii) where the Court cannot determine the amount of that duty, a penalty not exceeding 500 penalty
-- units".
@export What is the most I can be fined for evading duty, in dollars (s 234(1)(a), (2)(a))?
GIVEN `the court can determine the duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court can work out the duty evaded
      `the duty` IS A NUMBER @desc The duty evaded, in dollars
      `a penalty unit in dollars` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of a penalty unit (Crimes Act s 4AA: 330 dollars before indexation)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined for evading duty` IS
    IF `the court can determine the duty`
    THEN 5 * `the duty`
    ELSE 500 * `a penalty unit in dollars`

@export What is the least a court can fine me for evading duty, in dollars, when it can determine the duty (s 234(2)(a)(i))?
GIVEN `the duty` IS A NUMBER @desc The duty evaded, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the least I can be fined for evading duty` IS
    2 * `the duty`

-- s 233BABAD: tobacco products imported "with the intention of defrauding the revenue" (s (1)) -- "imprisonment for not
-- more than 15 years, a fine not exceeding the amount worked out under subsection (5), or both"; (5): "30 times the amount
-- of that duty; or (b) otherwise--12,000 penalty units". Imported "reckless as to whether there would be defrauding of
-- the revenue" (s (2A)) -- "imprisonment for not more than 10 years"; (5A): "20 times the amount of that duty; or (b)
-- otherwise--8,000 penalty units".
DECLARE `My state of mind about the revenue` IS ONE OF
    `I intended to defraud the revenue`
    `I was reckless whether the revenue would be defrauded`
    `neither`

@export What is the longest I can be imprisoned for importing tobacco to defraud the revenue, in years (s 233BABAD(4), (4A))?
GIVEN `my state of mind` IS A `My state of mind about the revenue` @desc Whether you intended, or were reckless about, defrauding the revenue
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the longest I can be imprisoned for tobacco fraud, in years` IS
    CONSIDER `my state of mind`
    WHEN `I intended to defraud the revenue` THEN 15
    WHEN `I was reckless whether the revenue would be defrauded` THEN 10
    WHEN `neither` THEN 0

@export What is the most I can be fined for importing tobacco to defraud the revenue, in dollars (s 233BABAD(5), (5A))?
GIVEN `my state of mind` IS A `My state of mind about the revenue` @desc Whether you intended, or were reckless about, defrauding the revenue
      `the court can determine the duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court can work out the duty that would have been payable
      `the duty` IS A NUMBER @desc The duty that would have been payable, in dollars
      `a penalty unit in dollars` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of a penalty unit (Crimes Act s 4AA: 330 dollars before indexation)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined for tobacco fraud` IS
    CONSIDER `my state of mind`
    WHEN `I intended to defraud the revenue` THEN
        IF `the court can determine the duty` THEN 30 * `the duty` ELSE 12000 * `a penalty unit in dollars`
    WHEN `I was reckless whether the revenue would be defrauded` THEN
        IF `the court can determine the duty` THEN 20 * `the duty` ELSE 8000 * `a penalty unit in dollars`
    WHEN `neither` THEN 0
